description.p:
As used herein, a "deπvative" of a specified microorganism is a progeny or other recipient microorganism that contains genetic mateπal obtained directly or indirectly from the specified microorganism. Such a deπvative microorganism may, for example, be formed by removing genetic mateπal from a specified microorganism and subsequently introducing it into another microorganism (i.e., the progeny or other recipient microorganism) by any conventional methodology including, but not limited to, transformation, conjugation, electroporation, transduction and the like. A deπvative may be formed by introducing one or more mutations into the genome of a microorganism. The mutations may be insertions into the genome of the microorganism. Alternatively, the mutations may be deletions of one or more bases from the genome of the microorganism. In some instances, the mutations may be the alteration of one or more bases in the genome of the microorganism. In addition, one microorganism is a deπvative of a parent microorganism if it contains the genome of the parent microorganism but does not contain the same extrachromosomal nucleic acid molecules. Thus, a strain 

produced by cuπng endogenous vectors from a parent strain is a deπvative of the parent strain. Examples of mutations or other genetic alterations which may be incorporated into the microorganisms of the present invention include, but are not limited to, mutations or alterations that create: a recA genotype such as recAl/recA13 or recA deletions, a lacL genotype that allows alpha complementation such as lacX74 ZαcZΔM15 or other lacL deletion, a protease deficient genotype such as Alon and/or ompY, an endonuclease minus genotype such as end Al, a genotype suitable for Ml 3 phage infection by including the F' episome, a restπction negative, modification positive genotype such as hsdR17(rκ , mκ+), a restπction negative, modification negative genotype such as hsdS20(r , 

 ), a methylase deficient genotype such as mcrA and/or mcrB and/or mrr, a genotype suitable for taking up large plasmids such as deoR, a genotype containing suppressor mutations such as supE and/or supF. Other suitable modifications are known to those skilled in the art and such modifications are considered to be within the scope of the present invention.
